(Bug #36 - a width specifier used to reinterpret a value's raw 64 bits as
 an integer: a float printed its IEEE-754 bit pattern and a text printed
 the string's ADDRESS. The type-aware dispatch was gated on there being
 no width, so writing a width skipped it entirely.

 The two same-content texts are the control that makes the diagnosis
 unambiguous: before the fix they printed DIFFERENT numbers, which no
 value-based explanation survives. They must print the same thing now.

 The number and boolean rows pass on both sides of the fix and are kept
 deliberately - they are what prove the integer width path still works
 and was not thrown out with the bug.

 Note the width is not APPLIED to a float or text: there is no
 string/float padding primitive in coreasm. The value is correct and
 unpadded, matching what the runtime-tagged `value` path already does.)
a float called f is 3.5.
a text called t is "hi".
a text called u is "hi".
a buffer called b is "buf".
a number called n is 42.
a boolean called ready is true.

Print "{f:06}".
Print "{f}".
Print "{f:.2}".
Print "{t:06}".
Print "{u:06}".
Print "{b:06}".
Print "{n:06}".
Print "{n:6}".
Print "{ready:06}".
